WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.700
Despite television being a very crowded field
right now, the police procedural series NCIS

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.090
is still going strong in its 18th season — and
even today it's one of the most-watched shows

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:12.090
in America.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:16.790
Loyal viewers have stuck with the series since
its premiere in 2003, and in its tenth season,

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:21.430
it even surpassed Sunday Night Football and
American Idol to become the highest rated

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.590
broadcast on network TV.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.510
Since that time, NCIS has never left the list
of top-three most watched shows in the country.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:34.210
It even inspired two spin-offs of its own
— NCIS: New Orleans and NCIS: Los Angeles,

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.219
both of which are hugely popular in their
own right.

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:41.399
Unsurprisingly for a massively successful
show that's been on the air for nearly two

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:45.980
decades, NCIS has also seen its fair share
of behind-the-scenes drama.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.850
Perhaps most contentious of all, however,
was showrunner and creator Donald Bellisario's

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.839
sudden departure from the show in 2007.

00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:57.920
After less than five years, why would someone
leave such a successful show, and one they

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:58.920
created, too?

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.269
Well, let's just say it wasn't exactly by
choice.

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:05.401
It's worth knowing a little more about Bellisario's
background in television — after all, his

00:01:05.401 --> 00:01:07.829
name is practically synonymous with the medium.

00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:12.260
His writing career began in 1977, when he
earned his first credit for penning an episode

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.690
of the Cliff Potts-led series Big Hawaii on
NBC.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:20.190
He continued to bolster his resume throughout
the late '70s as a writer and director, contributing

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.520
to some of the most well-known TV shows of
the decade, including Kojak, Battlestar Galactica,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:25.780
and Quincy, M.E.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.420
Bellisario's big break arrived in 1980, when
he launched his first series, Magnum P.I.,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.189
which became a cultural staple for American
TV and established Tom Selleck as a household

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:36.189
name.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:40.140
The show was an enormous success for CBS and
remained on the air for eight seasons.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.829
This was just the beginning of Bellisario's
working relationship with the network, however,

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:46.899
which would continue on and off for nearly
40 years.

00:01:46.899 --> 00:01:51.369
Despite the runaway success of Magnum, P.I.,
Bellisario's name wasn't exactly a golden

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:53.840
ticket to success for his TV creations.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.960
Tales of the Golden Monkey, an ABC adventure
show attempting to capitalize on the success

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.960
of Raiders of the Lost Ark, aired for only
one season in 1982-83.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.690
Another one of his works was Tequila and Bonetti,
a CBS comedy-drama about an Italian cop and

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.410
his talking canine partner.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.960
Amid poor reviews from critics, the network
cancelled the show before the last two of

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.090
its 12 filmed episodes could air.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:19.020
Still, after that rough patch he rebounded
with more hits, including the cult favorite

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.740
science fiction series Quantum Leap, which
debuted in 1989.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.900
The show survived for four seasons, with the
final episode attracting almost 13 million

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:27.900
viewers.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.500
But his biggest success, even above Magnum
P.I., would come in 1995, with the debut of

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.500
JAG.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.860
NBC is probably still kicking themselves to
this day over that one — only the first

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.160
season of JAG aired on their network, and
when they opted to drop the show, CBS swooped

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.160
in.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.060
What followed was a popular 10 season run.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.430
But more importantly, thanks to a backdoor
pilot in season 8, NCIS was born, spinning

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.730
off to quickly become the juggernaut franchise
it is today.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:58.400
But Bellisario's time with NCIS came to a
dramatic end when, in 2007, the show's star

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.060
Mark Harmon forced him out.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.080
According to reports, the two butted heads
for months, with Harmon disliking Bellisario's,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.090
quote, "chaotic management style" and the
long hours that resulted from it.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.930
The network decided Harmon's star power was
more important to the show than Bellisario's

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.360
behind-the-scenes work, and the latter was
subsequently fired.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.980
However, he retained an executive producer
title on the show, which he still holds today.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.980
This wasn't the end of the drama involving
Bellisario, though.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.550
The ex-showrunner sued CBS after NCIS: Los
Angeles entered into production.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.900
An existing clause in his contract gave him
the right to create the show's first spinoff,

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.110
but after his forced departure from the show,
CBS hired fellow NCIS Executive Producer Shane

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.590
Brennan to make it instead.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.600
Needless to say, Bellisario was not okay with
this.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.370
"Everything is not okay!"

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.500
Bellisario and CBS ultimately settled out
of court for an undisclosed amount.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.930
Bellisario is now retired from TV — and
although he may no longer be actively involved

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:56.460
with NCIS, fans still have him to thank for
creating and producing such a long-running

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.220
and beloved series.

